{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species","id":1546695,"title":"What if you base a story around killing invasive species?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-12-06T10:38:43-08:00","id":1546695,"title":"What if you base a story around killing invasive species?","content":"Like say that you're hunting things like Nutria in the Louisiana Bayou, Feral Cats, Super Pigs, Iguanas in Florida or Puerto Rico, Raccoon Dogs in Eastern Europe or Hippos in Columbia?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28613,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","user":"Samuelalpha86","date":". 12-06-2024, 03:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-06T15:25:02-08:00","id":1546705,"title":"","content":"I see two main ways of dealing with this:<br /> <br /> A short part of a bigger story where the PCs decide to deal with an invasive species. Fortunately the Garou are really good at killing things, and normal animals don't stand much of a chance (not just in direct combat, but with supernatural powers that can help summon animals/etc.). The biggest problem is making sure humans don't get too curious as to why a dug in invasive species is suddenly dying off.<br /> <br /> A bigger story of Garou politics as there's a lot to debate on how to approach invasive species. Some Garou are going to want to protect the local ecosystem, some Garou are going to accept changes as part of Gaia's plan (invasive species have been spreading long before humans came into being, humans just make it harder for this to occur without major disruptions) and be against killing innocent animals trying to live their lives, and some Garou are going to advocate for the Wyld and want to use invasive species to attack the Weaver's oppressive order. The PCs start on at least &quot;killing some to protect the Caern,&quot; side of things and the game unfolds from there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-06T15:33:54-08:00","id":1546706,"title":"","content":"Honestly, the only sane answer would be to kill the invasive species to preserve the local species. But many Garou like the Red Talons are not sane.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-06T15:57:40-08:00","id":1546707,"title":"","content":"While not necessarily inappropriate for discussing the Garou, that's very lacking in nuance regarding how to handle invasive species. Especially given some of your examples are of species that have had a long time to be part of a &quot;new normal&quot; for an area, just killing them causes lots of problems if you don't follow up with an already prepared introduction program of all the local species that are in trouble. And that's a delicate and complicated process that takes a lot of people and a lot of patience... not so much the Garou's strong suits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-06T16:00:24-08:00","id":1546708,"title":"","content":"How does killing an invasive species cause harm to the native species? From what I understand keeping them around causes more harm.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28613,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","user":"Samuelalpha86","date":".  details 12-06-2024, 07:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-06T20:42:24-08:00","id":1546715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546706#post1546706\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, the only sane answer would be to kill the invasive species to preserve the local species. But many Garou like the Red Talons are not sane.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you might not understand what their drive is, especially with respect to this topic and how it pertains the threatening species with anthropogenic exintction, i,e, the thing that their patron holds against humanity and why they don't accept homid Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T02:03:41-08:00","id":1546717,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546708#post1546708\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How does killing an invasive species cause harm to the native species? From what I understand keeping them around causes more harm.</div> </div> </div> </div>Killing the invasive species can can all sorts of problems that negatively impact the native ecosystem.<br /> <br /> I'm going to go for a plant example because it's easier:<br /> <br /> The tree of heaven, aka <i>Ailanthus altissima</i> pretty much puts a lot of the invasive animals to shame with how much is messes up new ecosystems. It can reproduce both sexually and asexually, there's nothing that kills it that doesn't kill everything else around it, it poisons other plants around it, and nothing in the US or Europe that will eat it eats it fast enough to matter.<br /> <br /> The entire tree is poisonous to other plants: leaves to roots. Every time you cut one down, you're spreading deadly leaves around the nearby plants. The roots leech extra poison into the ground when the tree is under stress. Taking it down to the stump without other efforts doesn't kill it, you have to cut it down, drill into the stump and pour bleach into the holes, and repeat that multiple seasons to kill a single plant... and then all the poison roots are still there, and can clone new trees.<br /> <br /> So, if you just run around kill the trees? You're left with lifeless soil that can't grow anything, you've poisoned most of the nearby plants that would try to recolonize the soil so the area of devastation gets even bigger, topsoil erosion ramps up, and so on. So, yeah, you need to be a lot more careful about what you do: you have to be prepared to do soil remediation, artificial root systems to keep the soil from eroding, and so on.<br /> <br /> It's important to remember that once an ecosystem in relative equilibrium is destabilized, nature does not resort to the previous state on its own. Disruptions impact different organisms at different rates. So when you remove the disruption, things will recover at different rates. And that leads to rapid booms and busts in the populations of native species that can be unrecoverable. You have to take a holistic approach to these things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T07:37:57-08:00","id":1546724,"title":"","content":"The Tree of Heaven is nothing like the species I gave as examples and seems like an extreme case. Anyways what you mean is that you have to fix the damage caused by the invasive species after you kill it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T07:39:16-08:00","id":1546725,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546715#post1546715\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think you might not understand what their drive is, especially with respect to this topic and how it pertains the threatening species with anthropogenic exintction, i,e, the thing that their patron holds against humanity and why they don't accept homid Garou.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the Red Talons thought that the Bunyip Tribe killed the Wolves in Australia which is bullshit since Australia never had wolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T10:12:35-08:00","id":1546732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546725#post1546725\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, the Red Talons thought that the Bunyip Tribe killed the Wolves in Australia which is bullshit since Australia never had wolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Aussie Talons were described as coming from the dumbest dumb dumbs that all Red Talons wanted to be rid of, but this is Rage Across Australia we're talking about. You've got to take everything with an entire salt dome. This was back when there was a seeming drive to write every Shadow Lord with a mustache so that they could twirl it menacingly, and the text was playing up Black Furies slaying male children even though that's something they stopped doing back in the Dark Ages. Edge for edge's sake, all of it.<br /> <br /> Also, to counter that on the Talons' front, the Red Talons in Africa and, by implication, India made contact with the Mokole of their respective territories and voluntarily shed their wolf forms for African wild painted dogs and dholes, respectively. You shouldn't take RaA to be the entirety of the game; it gets you nothing but W5.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T11:26:37-08:00","id":1546736,"title":"","content":"What is your goal in this? Do you want to run a chronicle about taking out invasive species, or do you want to use it as a way to showcase why most garou aren't sane?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://ana-mizuki.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">My gallery.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/110-ana-mizuki","name":"Ana Mizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar110_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2484"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T12:05:47-08:00","id":1546738,"title":"","content":"It's just an idea for a Story to put in a Chronicle nothing more. The Chronicle can have all sorts of things happen besides that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T12:28:03-08:00","id":1546740,"title":"","content":"I'm 100% behind Garou taking out as many feral cats, foxes, pigs etc. as they possibly can here in Australia. <br /> <br /> But as said above RaA is trash.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T15:24:41-08:00","id":1546745,"title":"","content":"I think as a chronicle element, it's fine. It works with the themes of the game. I think the real question is how do you make this gameable? Whether the Garou PCs succeed or fail, what are the complications that occur, what are the major story beats (plots), who are the antagonists, what important decisions do the PCs have to make, and so on. <br /> <br /> These would be the points I think the chronicle would need to cover.<br /> <br /> 1) By definition, extirpating an invasive species from an area is hard. There's a reason humans haven't been able to do so. Killing individual members of that species, even lots of them, is easy. Eliminating them entirely is very hard. Even if the Garou are able to kill them more easily than humans and kill even more of them, it doesn't necessarily follow Garou will be more successful overall. So put a lot of thought as to what constitutes success and how it can be achieved.<br /> <br /> 2) The main part of the game is for the Garou to fight supernatural evil, not mundane threats. An overemphasis on an issue like this would lead the Garou to neglect more pressing (and fun for the players) issues and opponents. So there needs to be a balance between plots like this and other plots and antagonists.<br /> <br /> 3) Resolution on this issue (just like most kinds of real world environmental issues) should involve the mobilization and involvement of Kinfolk. It doesn't matter that Garou are big combat monsters and can kill feral pigs very good. They can't kill all the feral pigs. You want to involve Kinfolk who can go on hunts with mortal hunters, be elected to local office, staff positions in relevant government agencies, become activists who rally the local community, and take other actions that Garou - by themselves - are poor at. So I'd really emphasize the PCs making good use of their own Kinfolk backgrounds and Kinfolk NPCs in the greater setting. That will involve making use of their character sheet as well as internal politicking within the sept. That should be a critical element.<br /> <br /> 4) Think of the spiritual impacts of this in the Umbra. What are the spirits of these invasive species doing in the local Penumbra? What spirit broods are they a part of? Which of the other spirits don't like them, do like them, or are indifferent? And remember that simply because a species is invasive in this particular area, it should not automatically override the normal spirit &quot;politics&quot; in the area based on the spirit broods. Feral pigs aren't boars. But most likely the Boar totem, and thus packs of boar, are still fond of them and might get angry that his children are being killed en masse. Nutria, being a rodent, could very well be a member of Rat's brood, and Rat mostly cares about survival, tolerates his children being very fecund, etc. So Rat might become upset as well as packs of Rat and the entire local Bone Gnawer tribe. They simply don't care if Nutria is an invasive species. They'll support them. What if the spirit of the invasive species is actually now a caern totem of a local sept (even if small)? What about other spirits, equally invasive and not native, but which may not be as destructive like certain species. Mustangs and feral horses are certainly not native to the Americas and even have controlled hunts on their population, but many people don't view them as being invasive. Lots of non-native birds have also fit into the local eco-system. So how do spirits like these and other introduced species view any Garou who have dedicated themselves to wiping out non-native species?<br /> <br /> While I think there may be a temptation to ignore complications like this, I think this will actually be the heart of dealing with this issue in the chronicle. The PCs will have to decide how to deal with Garou opponents who have their own reasons to oppose them. How do they deal with that? With violence? With compromise? With making political deals with non-involved Garou to outmaneuver them? What if such opponents are from different septs? And for the spirits, you are going to deal with issues of chiminage, proper use of rites like the Rite of Contrition and Prayer for the Prey, spirit notoriety and affinity, complications with spirit broods, and really making spirits an important part of the setting and resolution of this conflict.<br /> <br /> 5) Think of further complications. Adding in some possible fomori versions is one option. But so is introducing gorgons or even kami of the species. That would be a more unusual, and thus more interesting, options for the PCs to deal with. The Wyld isn't Gaia and has its own goals and preferences. Personally, I would try to limit the involvement of the Wyrm on an issue like this because approaching it from a different angle allows you to deal with other setting elements that are often neglected, and will lead to more interesting scenes and resolutions.<br /> <br /> 6) If you do make the Wyrm involved in some way, be creative and make it ancillary to the issue. Don't create something so the PCs can kill it and have an easy win. Keep it limited to an interesting sidequest that is just another complication. Maybe a Wyrm faction is helping to hunt them by introducing tainted poison traps that helped kill off many of the wolves in the 19th century. Maybe they are capturing certain species for grotesque experiments to create destructive fomori, or as guardians of important lairs. A remote Setite temple and its vampire priest might use these fomori guardians, but otherwise be uninterested in the invasive species. Or Gangrel vampires are also hunting them as an easy way to access blood they need to survive.<br /> <br /> You don't want complications that simply piss all over the PCs and make them feel they have no impact. Buy you want complications that make sense and provide a sense of drama and accomplishment as the PCs navigate the issue.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T16:02:14-08:00","id":1546748,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546724#post1546724\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anyways what you mean is that you have to fix the damage caused by the invasive species after you kill it?</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I mean that this is a complex undertaking. It's not just &quot;kill this, then fix that.&quot; You have to have a plan that incorporates the whole ecosystem in question if you want to return things to how they where. If you just want to kill the invasive species and let nature take its course, a lot of native species are probably going to die out as a whole new ecosystem emerges instead. If you want to restore an ecosystem you're going to have to cull some native species to keep them from overpopulating with the invasive species gone and their natural population controls not recovered yet, and you're going to have to work to get slower reproducing species back in place without compromising genetic stability. Etc.<br /> <br /> &quot;Life finds a way&quot; doesn't mean nature does what you want it to do. If you want to guide an ecosystem in a specific direction it takes management over time.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-07T16:30:18-08:00","id":1546750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546745#post1546745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think as a chronicle element, it's fine. It works with the themes of the game. I think the real question is how do you make this gameable? Whether the Garou PCs succeed or fail, what are the complications that occur, what are the major story beats (plots), who are the antagonists, what important decisions do the PCs have to make, and so on.<br /> <br /> These would be the points I think the chronicle would need to cover.<br /> <br /> 1) By definition, extirpating an invasive species from an area is hard. There's a reason humans haven't been able to do so. Killing individual members of that species, even lots of them, is easy. Eliminating them entirely is very hard. Even if the Garou are able to kill them more easily than humans and kill even more of them, it doesn't necessarily follow Garou will be more successful overall. So put a lot of thought as to what constitutes success and how it can be achieved.<br /> <br /> 2) The main part of the game is for the Garou to fight supernatural evil, not mundane threats. An overemphasis on an issue like this would lead the Garou to neglect more pressing (and fun for the players) issues and opponents. So there needs to be a balance between plots like this and other plots and antagonists.<br /> <br /> 3) Resolution on this issue (just like most kinds of real world environmental issues) should involve the mobilization and involvement of Kinfolk. It doesn't matter that Garou are big combat monsters and can kill feral pigs very good. They can't kill all the feral pigs. You want to involve Kinfolk who can go on hunts with mortal hunters, be elected to local office, staff positions in relevant government agencies, become activists who rally the local community, and take other actions that Garou - by themselves - are poor at. So I'd really emphasize the PCs making good use of their own Kinfolk backgrounds and Kinfolk NPCs in the greater setting. That will involve making use of their character sheet as well as internal politicking within the sept. That should be a critical element.<br /> <br /> 4) Think of the spiritual impacts of this in the Umbra. What are the spirits of these invasive species doing in the local Penumbra? What spirit broods are they a part of? Which of the other spirits don't like them, do like them, or are indifferent? And remember that simply because a species is invasive in this particular area, it should not automatically override the normal spirit &quot;politics&quot; in the area based on the spirit broods. Feral pigs aren't boars. But most likely the Boar totem, and thus packs of boar, are still fond of them and might get angry that his children are being killed en masse. Nutria, being a rodent, could very well be a member of Rat's brood, and Rat mostly cares about survival, tolerates his children being very fecund, etc. So Rat might become upset as well as packs of Rat and the entire local Bone Gnawer tribe. They simply don't care if Nutria is an invasive species. They'll support them. What if the spirit of the invasive species is actually now a caern totem of a local sept (even if small)? What about other spirits, equally invasive and not native, but which may not be as destructive like certain species. Mustangs and feral horses are certainly not native to the Americas and even have controlled hunts on their population, but many people don't view them as being invasive. Lots of non-native birds have also fit into the local eco-system. So how do spirits like these and other introduced species view any Garou who have dedicated themselves to wiping out non-native species?<br /> <br /> While I think there may be a temptation to ignore complications like this, I think this will actually be the heart of dealing with this issue in the chronicle. The PCs will have to decide how to deal with Garou opponents who have their own reasons to oppose them. How do they deal with that? With violence? With compromise? With making political deals with non-involved Garou to outmaneuver them? What if such opponents are from different septs? And for the spirits, you are going to deal with issues of chiminage, proper use of rites like the Rite of Contrition and Prayer for the Prey, spirit notoriety and affinity, complications with spirit broods, and really making spirits an important part of the setting and resolution of this conflict.<br /> <br /> 5) Think of further complications. Adding in some possible fomori versions is one option. But so is introducing gorgons or even kami of the species. That would be a more unusual, and thus more interesting, options for the PCs to deal with. The Wyld isn't Gaia and has its own goals and preferences. Personally, I would try to limit the involvement of the Wyrm on an issue like this because approaching it from a different angle allows you to deal with other setting elements that are often neglected, and will lead to more interesting scenes and resolutions.<br /> <br /> 6) If you do make the Wyrm involved in some way, be creative and make it ancillary to the issue. Don't create something so the PCs can kill it and have an easy win. Keep it limited to an interesting sidequest that is just another complication. Maybe a Wyrm faction is helping to hunt them by introducing tainted poison traps that helped kill off many of the wolves in the 19th century. Maybe they are capturing certain species for grotesque experiments to create destructive fomori, or as guardians of important lairs. A remote Setite temple and its vampire priest might use these fomori guardians, but otherwise be uninterested in the invasive species. Or Gangrel vampires are also hunting them as an easy way to access blood they need to survive.<br /> <br /> You don't want complications that simply piss all over the PCs and make them feel they have no impact. Buy you want complications that make sense and provide a sense of drama and accomplishment as the PCs navigate the issue.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, killing invasive species would be more of a sidequest for a story or two, searching for them is more or less the same as hunting in game, and killing a lot of them counts as a success. I have thought that in the case of killing Feral Cats bothering Cat spirits then something can be done as an apology if someone is associated with such a spirit. I have also thought of the possibility of a Hippo Fomor resulting from the pollution and death the species is causing in Columbia. Involving Kinfolk also sounds like a good idea.<br /> <br /> Also, Horses are fine. They won't be a focus.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Oj5beC8luic?si=EEPQ0zLHdXKon-Ph\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/google-material/image_placeholder.svg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Oj5beC8luic?si=EEPQ0zLHdXKon-Ph\" target=\"_blank\"> </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://youtu.be/Oj5beC8luic?si=EEPQ0zLHdXKon-Ph\" target=\"_blank\"> https://youtu.be/Oj5beC8luic?si=EEPQ0zLHdXKon-Ph </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28613,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","user":"Samuelalpha86","date":". 12-08-2024, 02:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-08T08:29:55-08:00","id":1546764,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546748#post1546748\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, I mean that this is a complex undertaking. It's not just &quot;kill this, then fix that.&quot; You have to have a plan that incorporates the whole ecosystem in question if you want to return things to how they where. If you just want to kill the invasive species and let nature take its course, a lot of native species are probably going to die out as a whole new ecosystem emerges instead. If you want to restore an ecosystem you're going to have to cull some native species to keep them from overpopulating with the invasive species gone and their natural population controls not recovered yet, and you're going to have to work to get slower reproducing species back in place without compromising genetic stability. Etc.<br /> <br /> &quot;Life finds a way&quot; doesn't mean nature does what you want it to do. If you want to guide an ecosystem in a specific direction it takes management over time.</div> </div> </div> </div>How would a species multiply after having their existence threatened?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-08T11:18:58-08:00","id":1546766,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546764#post1546764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> How would a species multiply after having their existence threatened?</div> </div> </div> </div>We are talking about a food web with multiple linkages and knock-on effects here. Initial removal of an invasive species can have some native species take advantage of niches that are now unoccupied in places that the existing system didn't allow, and delay the restoration of the prior system or potentially make it impossible without further action, particularly if humans are around with their thumb on a scale that wasn't anticipated beforehand. I don't know how things work in AUS with regard to land ownership and these issues, but the US has a rather extensive problem with the intersection of private property laws and endangered species protections, to take one example.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-08T14:39:30-08:00","id":1546771,"title":"","content":"Sometimes the harm is a species multiplying too much.<br /> <br /> Let's remember that humans are also an incredibly effective invasive species. The entire US east of the Mississippi river has a major problem: deer. Not that deer are dying, but they're vastly overpopulated because we humans killed all off (at least locally) the major terrestrial apex predators that used to eat them. Coyotes are starting to return (and in theory could replace wolves as the apex predator for deer), but with humans in the way coyote populations are far too low and far to inexperienced with hunting deer to keep the deer herds in check. The fact that the deer are local is actually part of the problem. The area is where they thrive, they're perfectly designed to eat and breed and repeat. And they have no concept of a need to stop; their instinct is to reproduce as much as possible before predators kill too many of them. If humans stopped managing deer herds... they'd explode out of control, strip whole grass lands and forests bare, killing hundreds of local species that needed those plants for their own survival... and then the deer populations would crash because there's no food left for the giant population that would have been produced without humans hunting them to take the place of wolves and cougars hunting them.<br /> <br /> It's really hard to describe the projections of what would happen if humans just decided to stop hunting deer and didn't do anything else about it. &quot;Dust bowl&quot; would seem quaint to the kind of damage we're talking about.<br /> <br /> This is one of the things that consistently infuriates me with how the books write about the Red Talons. They're perfectly OK with thinking like humans when it comes to justifying hate and violence, but completely opposed to thinking like humans when it comes to using their long term planning abilities to actually restore ecosystems to pre-human conditions,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-08T17:24:05-08:00","id":1546772,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546771#post1546771\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sometimes the harm is a species multiplying too much.<br /> <br /> Let's remember that humans are also an incredibly effective invasive species. The entire US east of the Mississippi river has a major problem: deer. Not that deer are dying, but they're vastly overpopulated because we humans killed all off (at least locally) the major terrestrial apex predators that used to eat them. Coyotes are starting to return (and in theory could replace wolves as the apex predator for deer), but with humans in the way coyote populations are far too low and far to inexperienced with hunting deer to keep the deer herds in check. The fact that the deer are local is actually part of the problem. The area is where they thrive, they're perfectly designed to eat and breed and repeat. And they have no concept of a need to stop; their instinct is to reproduce as much as possible before predators kill too many of them. If humans stopped managing deer herds... they'd explode out of control, strip whole grass lands and forests bare, killing hundreds of local species that needed those plants for their own survival... and then the deer populations would crash because there's no food left for the giant population that would have been produced without humans hunting them to take the place of wolves and cougars hunting them.<br /> <br /> It's really hard to describe the projections of what would happen if humans just decided to stop hunting deer and didn't do anything else about it. &quot;Dust bowl&quot; would seem quaint to the kind of damage we're talking about.<br /> <br /> This is one of the things that consistently infuriates me with how the books write about the Red Talons. They're perfectly OK with thinking like humans when it comes to justifying hate and violence, but completely opposed to thinking like humans when it comes to using their long term planning abilities to actually restore ecosystems to pre-human conditions,</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing is that humans are self-aware and know to control the Deer population but other animals don't. This includes Invasive species who destroy everything with no self-awareness, they don't fill a niche, they cause an imbalance by outcompeting local species.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-08T19:45:41-08:00","id":1546785,"title":"","content":"When I have incorporated invasive species in my Chronicles its the spiritual damage as well as the environmental damage. ie. The Pack were attempting to repair damaged Songlines and an sacred djang sites tied to the Banded Hare Wallaby Ancestor who was fading and its destruction would have pulled down the entire web of spiritual heritage for the area. They needed to work hard to permanently eradicate cats, foxes and rabbits from the area, assist with programs to reintroduce the species and assist Elders afflicted with Alzheimers and senility to recall and pass on Secret Sacred knowledge ( that the Garou themselves were not allowed to see.) to the few initiated folks left in the area.<br /> <br /> It was a long and hard struggle that was only a partial success in the end and cost one of the Pack their lives due to Harano.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/479-damian-may","name":"Damian May","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar479_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1199"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T07:36:41-08:00","id":1546794,"title":"","content":"I also hate the Red Talons' justification of hate since that's exactly what The Wyrm wants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T09:10:30-08:00","id":1546800,"title":"","content":"Do this, and then have a bunch of spirits from these invasive species your Garou were killing, becoming corrupted by the Wyrm. Then, next time the Garou go hunting, it won't be so easy - and now there's a huge problem for them to deal with, with said animals spreading corruption in the entire area. I would love to have this played out in a table.<br /> <br /> And yes, it is not really a good thing to be done irl","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T09:26:53-08:00","id":1546802,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Samuelalpha86</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546772#post1546772\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The thing is that humans are self-aware and know to control the Deer population but other animals don't. This includes Invasive species who destroy everything with no self-awareness, they don't fill a niche, they cause an imbalance by outcompeting local species.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nature has no balance. Lions will eat all prey and reproduce like crazy, until there's too many lions for too little pray. Then they'll all starve to death. That's what is called &quot;dynamic balance&quot; that is Nature. It is absolutely brutal. No organisms remain within some arcane &quot;sustainable&quot; boundaries because they are so fluffy. They all &quot;destroy&quot; their ecosystem, until that comes back to bite them in the ass, and they all die by the droves. That's how mother Nature keeps its balance: DEATH.<br /> <br /> Humans are actually the only species that goes against that - to the point that humans decide not to reproduce uncontrollably. But that's only a modern thing. Before the last half of the 20th century, there were only 3 ways that human populations would be kept in control: plagues, famine and wars. Which is why wars were always inevitable, despite of what some hippies may think. With modern technology, we may make war obsolete, but until now it was a necessity.<br /> <br /> As for other organisms, death is the only thing that keeps anyone of them in check. That can be done by predators, that can be done with internal competition - a wolf's worse enemy will always be another wolf, a lion's worse threat will always be another lion, and a deer's worse threat will always be another deer, because organisms of the same species compete for the same resources, including reproductive rights. Or it can be done by massive famine, after a species depletes an area of all the resources.<br /> <br /> If the deers eat all grass, they'll starve to death and their population will greatly decrease. Then, over time, the surviving grass will spread again, this time very fast because there's almost no deers to eat it. Then, there will be plenty grass for very few deers, thus the surviving deers will be in overabundance, meaning they'll start breeding like rats, until such a time that their population exceeds the sustainable levels. Then the grass disappears once again, they all die and the cycle continues. That's how Mother Nature works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T10:59:47-08:00","id":1546808,"title":"","content":"I know that. I said that animals are not self-aware and only act on instinct.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28613,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","user":"Samuelalpha86","date":". 12-09-2024, 02:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T13:40:23-08:00","id":1546815,"title":"","content":"The problem isn't self-awareness or instinct, but rather context. Most animals are self-aware, and humans are incredibly skilled at convincing animals to act outside of their normal instincts. But our history with incorporating other animals into our social systems has also taught us the importance of context when communicating with other animals. A dog is not born with the instinct to sit on command from a human. They have to be taught. Teaching them requires providing them contexts they do grasp instinctively and building from there (also this would be impossible without some level of self-awareness on the dog's part). <br /> <br /> Deer understand on some level that there is a time to stop eating and move on to green pastures. In the wild, they don't naturally strip biomes bare before moving on, because as herd animals they know food scarcity is a problem larger than one individual and they need to migrate while everyone is full instead of eating all the food possible. The context problem they have is what happens when there isn't a new feeding ground to move to that's abundant enough. That's when the problems start because the math changes. If, within their perceptional context, all places are scarce on food, they start stripping what they can find bare because there's no sure bet somewhere else. That's when the grass might never recover, or the deer might never recover, and all the knock on effects from that spread damage even wider.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T18:01:19-08:00","id":1546821,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Heavy Arms</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546815#post1546815\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem isn't self-awareness or instinct, but rather context. Most animals are self-aware, and humans are incredibly skilled at convincing animals to act outside of their normal instincts. But our history with incorporating other animals into our social systems has also taught us the importance of context when communicating with other animals. A dog is not born with the instinct to sit on command from a human. They have to be taught. Teaching them requires providing them contexts they do grasp instinctively and building from there (also this would be impossible without some level of self-awareness on the dog's part).<br /> <br /> Deer understand on some level that there is a time to stop eating and move on to green pastures. In the wild, they don't naturally strip biomes bare before moving on, because as herd animals they know food scarcity is a problem larger than one individual and they need to migrate while everyone is full instead of eating all the food possible. The context problem they have is what happens when there isn't a new feeding ground to move to that's abundant enough. That's when the problems start because the math changes. If, within their perceptional context, all places are scarce on food, they start stripping what they can find bare because there's no sure bet somewhere else. That's when the grass might never recover, or the deer might never recover, and all the knock on effects from that spread damage even wider.</div> </div> </div> </div>When that happens, it's usually locally. Yes, the grass may disappear from one location, causing all the deers to go &quot;extinct&quot; on that location... For a while.<br /> <br /> But there will still be grass and deers in other locations, and in time, they return. Or not. Extinction is also a pivotal part of Mother Nature. We ourselves are only here after all because 99.9% of every species that has ever walked on this planet went extinct. Without the meteor to wipe out the colossal Jurassic chickens, we wouldn't be here.<br /> <br /> Many species have many survival mechanisms, and diverse ones for that matter. Mammals have well developed brains that allows them a good level of cognitive capabilities, but that's an exception. Most organisms don't have that, but they have several other mechanisms. Even bacteria have chemical strategies - for example, developing protective &quot;carapaces&quot; of protein when the environment changes, which essentially puts them into &quot;hibernation&quot;. And several other schemes.<br /> <br /> But, oversimplifying, every organism consumes and reproduces as much as possible and until it is possible; there's no &quot;mother Gaia consciousness&quot; about &quot;sustainability&quot;. Survival evolutionary tactics to endure in hard times are the result of the weak dying to the harsh conditions, leaving the fittest alive to propagate the victorious genes that were more prone to survive. When deers migrate, it is to postpone the inevitable. Grass too will evolve to better survive - including to survive the predation from deers. Heck, kill all predators in north America and give it some 100 thousand years and you can be sure that some branches of deers will turn carnivore. Or a South American Puma will eventually migrate north. The &quot;Dynamic Balance&quot; always sets itself in the end. It's just a matter of time. After each one the major extinction events, happens an explosion of biodiversity. &quot;Invasive Species&quot; is just a new adaptation. Soon, they'll get in this dynamic balance within their new environment, and will start to diverge from their original branch.<br /> <br /> Sure, deers will probably migrate before all grass is gone; they evolved brains for that exact purpose. UNLESS there's nowhere new to migrate to. Then yes, they could all be gone. For good. That's extinction. And that would just simply open up the way for new species of grass and new species of herbivores to set root on the great plains, even if they need to cross the ocean to do so, and even if that takes hundreds of thousands of years.<br /> <br /> I am adamantly against the naturalist idiocy of thinking that &quot;mother nature&quot; is perfect. It is not. It is worse than cruel or brutal or violent. Because, if it were any of that, it would at least be a will that guides it.<br /> <br /> No, it is worse, because it is completely indifferent. For the unconscious &quot;mother Nature&quot;, if the entire planet Earth were swallowed by the Sun, it wouldn't make any difference. The universe wouldn't cry over the loss of all that biodiversity.<br /> <br /> That's why it's only we humans that truly matter. Because we are the only ones that care. The only ones that feel and that can bring meaning to things. Deers are pretty and fluffy little beasts, yes, but they wouldn't be making epic poems about the &quot;twilight of the Gods&quot; once they perceive that their extinction is imminent. They'll just simply suffer and then die from starvation up to their last member. Only we humans care. Which why we are special, despite the post modern tendency of self hate that has been propagated in the last decades.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-09T18:50:47-08:00","id":1546825,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546821#post1546821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am adamantly against the naturalist idiocy of thinking that &quot;mother nature&quot; is perfect. It is not. It is worse than cruel or brutal or violent. Because, if it were any of that, it would at least be a will that guides it.</div> </div> </div> </div>While this is true in real life, this is not a helpful perspective in the setting of Werewolf: the Apocalyse because there <i>are</i> wills that guide it: Gaia and the major Incarna. Gaia obviously allows the ecosystem to regulate itself according to natural principals, but in addition to that there is a lot of mystic mumbo-jumbo because the spirit world exists to guide the material world. <br /> <br /> Now an ST can choose to ignore that, but I think it'll create more problems for the ST than any problems that such a position solves. It is probably best if the ST depicts a kind of romantic spiritualism that leads to a planetary self-regulation absent the machinations of the Wyrm or other Triatic powers, even if Gaia herself is distant and somewhat unknowable.<br /> <br /> I suspect you even agree as this topic has gotten somewhat away from the game perspective in talking about real world ecology, but felt it was a good time to bring this back up.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-10T07:05:26-08:00","id":1546835,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546825#post1546825\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> While this is true in real life, this is not a helpful perspective in the setting of Werewolf: the Apocalyse because there <i>are</i> wills that guide it: Gaia and the major Incarna. Gaia obviously allows the ecosystem to regulate itself according to natural principals, but in addition to that there is a lot of mystic mumbo-jumbo because the spirit world exists to guide the material world.<br /> <br /> Now an ST can choose to ignore that, but I think it'll create more problems for the ST than any problems that such a position solves. It is probably best if the ST depicts a kind of romantic spiritualism that leads to a planetary self-regulation absent the machinations of the Wyrm or other Triatic powers, even if Gaia herself is distant and somewhat unknowable.<br /> <br /> I suspect you even agree as this topic has gotten somewhat away from the game perspective in talking about real world ecology, but felt it was a good time to bring this back up.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed it is. Yes, in the context of the game, you're absolutely right, and yes, we ended up derailing the topic into rl ecology.<br /> <br /> Back to it, I think that this could be a good way to end up having several of these &quot;invasive species&quot; being hunted down by a group of overzealous Garou, to become corrupted by the Wyrm and causing a much bigger problem then anticipated. I think this could be a very fun idea to play with if applied<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T02:03:54-08:00","id":1546918,"title":"","content":"For me, what could make an 'invading species' interesting, because frankly, werewolves should be able to wipe out any number of, for instance, deer, would be if those invaders were kinfolk for one of the other changing breeds - then you've got a nice potential for conflict, a chance for the werewolves to examine the past actions of their tribes, etc, etc.<br /> <br /> What drives these questions should be - what would make a good story?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-13T08:14:37-08:00","id":1546924,"title":"","content":"Well, I was thinking it would be more of a side-story.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T13:52:00-08:00","id":1547069,"title":"","content":"In my present chronicle I'm using the &quot;invasive species&quot; like a plot device. It's based in a real situation which occurring in Brazil. A hybrid of boar and domestic pig (it's calling javaporco), created to provide more meat than the common pig, become a invasive species and the government are give in hunting license to kill this animals and only this animals. Since 2023 it's become easy to members of a CAC (a association of hunters, sportiest shooter and gun collectors) get permission to buy and use shotguns, rifles and other type of guns specially if the forest areas in the CAC region have positive confirmation to the presence of javaporco. So, members of the CACs are purposely (and ilegal) introducing javaporco in areas when they not occur, then the hunters can get more guns and permissions to hunting. Of course, CACs are using this like a excuse to shoot native fauna, like de native wild boar and even great felines. And, some CACs members are starting to acting like vigilants killing people they believe are criminals. That's in the wod is obviously been use by the Wyrm minions to cause more suffering, promove the killing of species and have a militia to throw over the pack and the kinfolks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia","name":"Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10047_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T13:55:25-08:00","id":1547071,"title":"","content":"So basically, the Brazilian equivalent to North America's Super Pigs?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28613-samuelalpha86","name":"Samuelalpha86","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"470"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-16T21:08:42-08:00","id":1547090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1547069#post1547069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my present chronicle I'm using the &quot;invasive species&quot; like a plot device. It's based in a real situation which occurring in Brazil. A hybrid of boar and domestic pig (it's calling javaporco), created to provide more meat than the common pig, become a invasive species and the government are give in hunting license to kill this animals and only this animals. Since 2023 it's become easy to members of a CAC (a association of hunters, sportiest shooter and gun collectors) get permission to buy and use shotguns, rifles and other type of guns specially if the forest areas in the CAC region have positive confirmation to the presence of javaporco. So, members of the CACs are purposely (and ilegal) introducing javaporco in areas when they not occur, then the hunters can get more guns and permissions to hunting. Of course, CACs are using this like a excuse to shoot native fauna, like de native wild boar and even great felines. And, some CACs members are starting to acting like vigilants killing people they believe are criminals. That's in the wod is obviously been use by the Wyrm minions to cause more suffering, promove the killing of species and have a militia to throw over the pack and the kinfolks.</div> </div> </div> </div>Javaporcos occur naturally when pigs are set in the wilds and mix with the boars.<br /> <br /> I just wanted to know from where you're getting that fiction about the CACs acting as vigilantes, that was some nice fanfic you wrote there bro. I won't even tell you why that nonsense is pure delusion, I'll let you stop and think for a few minutes, and after that, I would stop listening to the idiotic nonsense that &quot;news&quot; such as Uol concocts (&quot;Uol&quot; is a group of propaganda who swears that they are a news organization from Brazil).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-17T00:02:30-08:00","id":1547094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1547090#post1547090\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Javaporcos occur naturally when pigs are set in the wilds and mix with the boars.<br /> <br /> I just wanted to know from where you're getting that fiction about the CACs acting as vigilantes, that was some nice fanfic you wrote there bro. I won't even tell you why that nonsense is pure delusion, I'll let you stop and think for a few minutes, and after that, I would stop listening to the idiotic nonsense that &quot;news&quot; such as Uol concocts (&quot;Uol&quot; is a group of propaganda who swears that they are a news organization from Brazil).</div> </div> </div> </div>You appear to be very very hurt here... but, this forum it's not a placer to discuss this... like I say, there are news about this, the only thing I invented is the wyrm action.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10047-vis\u00e3o-de-\u00e1guia","name":"Vis\u00e3o-de-\u00c1guia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10047_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-12-17T01:39:54-08:00","id":1547100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1546695-what-if-you-base-a-story-around-killing-invasive-species?p=1546821#post1546821\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I am adamantly against the naturalist idiocy of thinking that &quot;mother nature&quot; is perfect. It is not. It is worse than cruel or brutal or violent. Because, if it were any of that, it would at least be a will that guides it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Straw man argument. The actual arguments that you were encountering were that there are complex interactions of biotic and abiotic factors that are in play.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> No, it is worse, because it is completely indifferent. For the unconscious &quot;mother Nature&quot;, if the entire planet Earth were swallowed by the Sun, it wouldn't make any difference. The universe wouldn't cry over the loss of all that biodiversity. </div> </div> </div>Irrelevant.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That's why it's only we humans that truly matter. </div> </div> </div>You're in the wrong forum to express that sentiment with any degree of comfort. Mostly because with the cracking and smashing that pedestal has taken, it's awfully precarious to try and sit atop it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Because we are the only ones that care. The only ones that feel and that can bring meaning to things. Deers are pretty and fluffy little beasts, yes, but they wouldn't be making epic poems about the &quot;twilight of the Gods&quot; once they perceive that their extinction is imminent. They'll just simply suffer and then die from starvation up to their last member. Only we humans care. Which why we are special, despite the post modern tendency of self hate that has been propagated in the last decades. </div> </div> </div>Empathy has been recorded in no small number of other species, including those typically discounted by humans for not being mammals. Logging behavior has gotten way easier and old sentiments have broken down their institutional entrenchment as discoveries continue to hit them hard; the triune brain nonsense, for example, has been reeling for a while, but the death blows came in actually looking at different brains instead of just assuming shit.<br /> <br /> So I don't think that only humans care. Only humans have managed to game the system for the energy to support a single point mutation in a gene that caused our basal radioglia counts to triple, which made social management way easier and facilitated substantial increases in transmission of intergenerational knowledge, but this doesn't make us special for having capacities. Only for cranking them forward, which any mutations might or might not grant at random to any species. Since it also comes with the potential for intergenerational entrenchment of &quot;knowledge&quot; that's actually damned fucking wrong, you might want to loosen your belt about things a bit.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}